🤖 **Comment from Claude working with Aaron Silverman:**

**[P1]** For opted-out subjects, also skip allocations that randomize between variations, not only `doLog=true` allocations.

`excludes` returns true only when `do_log` is true. An allocation that splits traffic between variations without logging keeps randomizing opted-out subjects. Examples are an ordinary percentage split and a concluded experiment that still splits (see my comment on #139368 at `ufc.go:179`). We want opted-out subjects to skip randomization. The RFC reuses `doLog` alone, so this needs a product decision.

I suggest the compiler mark the allocations that randomize, and the evaluators skip `doLog || randomized`. The compiler already analyzes split coverage (`ufc/partition_coverage.go:88`), which is a better base for this check than three evaluators. The patch below is a prototype of the other option, inferring it in the three evaluators. It shows the behavior change and passes the suites, but it is not exact (see the fold).

<details>
<summary><b>Evidence</b> — opted-out subjects before and after the prototype</summary>

A `doLog=false` allocation that splits 50/50 between `a` and `b`, then a fallback. 200 opted-out subjects in immediate mode; the JSON and archived evaluators agree on every subject.

| | `a` | `b` | fallback |
|---|---|---|---|
| `7a1f3c52` | 106 | 94 | 0 |
| with the prototype | 0 | 0 | 200 |

With the prototype, `//domains/ffe/libs/flagging/ufc:go_default_test`, `//domains/ffe/libs/flagging/rust/evaluation:test` and `//domains/ffe/apps/edge-assignments:test` pass. That includes Go `TestEvaluationSDKOptOutCapabilities` and Rust `shared_opt_out_capabilities_match_json_and_archive`, which compare each shared opt-out case with its expected result.

</details>

<details>
<summary><b>Where the prototype is not exact</b></summary>

It skips splits with an empty shard and stops at the first unsharded split. Two cases with an unreachable alternative serve `a` with it: an unsharded `a` split before a sharded `b` split, and a `b` split whose shard has no ranges. It still counts a split as reachable after an `a` split whose shard covers the full range through several ranges, so it would wrongly skip that allocation. The optimizer keeps that shape, because `isNoopShard` checks single ranges (`ufc/optimizer.go:171-177`). A compiler-side check can handle this case, but it needs an ordered analysis of which variations are reachable; `splitsCoverAllPartitions` checks coverage only, so it is a starting point, not the whole check.

</details>

<details>
<summary><b>Open questions</b> — future-only mode, rollouts, shared fixtures</summary>

1. Ordinary allocations have no `randomizationActivatedAt`. Under this rule, future-only mode would skip every randomized ordinary allocation, even one that started before the cutoff. #139381 would need to record activation for any randomized allocation.
2. A `doLog=false` rollout of one variation behind a percentage gate does not choose between variations, so this rule keeps it eligible. That is a product call too.
3. Add shared fixture rows, so every SDK that evaluates locally gets the same rule.

</details>

